-§6.4 calls its list initial, to be refined during implementation; a refinement is a change to this file as above, never an unlisted point in a conditional workflow. §6.4's "additional checks at the inspect and test steps" is two points here, one per step, so that a check names the step it runs at. The two points divide between them every check and review the delegation loop makes of a change, wherever the module's text places it and whatever the change is: an increment, a fix, a refactoring candidate, or the gate pass of a stage's refactoring branch. The test step is the running of the tests (the increment's tests and the suite, the new tests against the checkpoint) and the checks that run with them, such as the observability tests and the benchmark floor; step 6 runs no tests of its own, the tests having run in step 4, and the trait *performance budgets* carries the floor (§6.8). The inspect step is every other check or review, before the tests or after them: the scope check, the diagram check, the boundary log, the triage of the builder's assumptions, the reviewer passes (such as the design auditor the trait *has screens* carries), the secret scan and the dependency admission. The merge gate's conditions (founding rule N6; the delegation loop applies them at its step 8) belong to the core and offer no point; a CI check a conditional workflow needs is added at `configuration.items`. An addition at either point is placed in the delegation loop's text at the step that runs it. Two points are added to §6.4's list, both at intake, because §6.5 names their kinds as values a type workflow or sub-workflow sets and the core settles both at intake, before design and planning act on them: `intake.budget-kind`, the kind of performance budget, and `intake.delivery-kind`, the delivery kind; `project-end.delivery` holds the procedure for the kind set there. The delivery kinds are one list, the kinds `Delivery-Conventions.md` §1 ships plus §6.4's own: `installer` (a desktop application, §2), `install-script` (a command-line tool or a local server, §3), `package` (a library, its registry's artifact), `web-deployment` (a hosted web application), `store-package` (an app delivered through a store) and `host-installation` (an extension installed into its host); the value set at `intake.delivery-kind` and the kind an addition at `project-end.delivery` names are both drawn from it, and it grows only as a point does, and workflow-check.py's own copy of it (`DELIVERY_KINDS`) changes with it. Where no type workflow or sub-workflow sets a part's kind, the kind the supervisor confirms with the owner at intake (Phase 1) is the part's kind, recorded by its key from this list. Each addition at `project-end.delivery` names the delivery kind it is for and applies only to a part of that kind; so a sub-workflow that sets another kind leaves its type workflow's procedure behind, and a trait's procedure for one kind never reaches a part of another. A completion condition added at `project-end.completion-conditions` is checked at project end; a condition the plan must build toward is also added at intake (`intake.requirement-sources`), naming that completion condition as its source, so that it enters the requirements register before planning.
